  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "How to read this diagram",
      "items": [
        "This is a sequence: time flows top to bottom. Each column is a part of the engine; each arrow is a call or a reply.",
        "It follows ONE turn end to end. A returning arrow is a response coming back.",
        "Click a column header's SRC badge to see the exact code behind that part."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "Glossary (plain words)",
      "items": [
        "Turn - one request handled end to end by app/engine/runtime.py.",
        "Topic - the named behaviour a message routes to.",
        "Hydrator / effector - a read-only tool vs. an action that changes something.",
        "Maker-checker - a high-risk action waits for a second approver before it runs.",
        "PII tokenise/restore - swap personal data for placeholders before the model, restore only for the allowed audience."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Guided tour - one turn",
      "items": [
        "1. The caller asks the engine to run a turn.",
        "2. The engine governs it (kill switch + quotas), then runs input guardrails.",
        "3. It routes to a topic and assembles context (knowledge + memory).",
        "4. It asks the model to reason-act; the reply comes back.",
        "5. A high-risk effector suspends for approval; output guardrails re-mask; the reply is returned."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Why the checks exist",
      "items": [
        "govern runs first so a killed or over-quota turn does no work.",
        "guardrails wrap the model in code, not prompt text, so injection/PII are deterministic.",
        "maker-checker: the model proposes a high-risk action, a human approves it - the model never self-approves.",
        "guardrails-out re-masks so PII reaches only an allowed audience."
      ]
    }
  ]
}